WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:09.410
Hi Sarah!

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.360
I’m Marcia Boardman – we spoke on the
phone...

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.110
Oh yes, I remember – I was having a bad
day!

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:16.800
...and this is Philip Hart, our CEO.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.800
Hello!

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.020
Hello, it’s very nice to meet you both.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:26.100
It says on your CV that you do voluntary work...

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.140
Well, when I have time, yes!

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.750
I do work at a centre for children with difficulties,
yes...

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.100
That must be very interesting...

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:36.010
It’s rewarding and challenging.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:39.190
Sarah, can you tell us a little bit about
your current position?

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.670
Sarah: I’m assistant sales director for
a chain of language schools.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.149
So this post would be quite a change then...

00:00:46.149 --> 00:00:50.940
I don’t think so honestly because the skills
are the same, despite the product.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.710
Why are you thinking of moving on?

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:59.219
Well I’ve come as far as I can in my current
position, I feel and I’m 28 now and would

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:02.020
love to take on some more responsibility.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.590
Ok, let’s move on to managing people.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.930
Can you give us an example of when you had
to deal with a particularly difficult managee?

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:16.160
Well, yes, I had two people working for me
who didn’t get along at all – they had

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:22.360
no shared values, different temperaments – and
they used to argue a lot, it looked like it

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.759
was impossible for them to work together.

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:31.110
So, I sat down with them, and we talked about
their differences and their problems and after

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.460
that, things went much more smoothly!

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.310
How would you deal with a rapidly changing
and uncertain global market?

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:43.330
Ok, that’s actually something I wanted to
talk about in my presentation...but I’d

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:48.399
say to stay flexible and to diversify markets
and sales strategies.

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:52.780
It says on your CV you speak French and Spanish.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:53.850
Yes, I do.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.040
I lived in both France and Spain for a while.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.110
Any plans to learn other languages?

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.840
I’d love to try!

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.629
Ok, can you tell us about a time you had to
close a particularly challenging deal?

00:02:06.629 --> 00:02:11.599
Well, the biggest contract I won - and the
thing I’m most proud of professionally - was

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:15.910
with a large university in India, to provide
language training.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:20.660
The contract was full of technicalities and
the client was very picky!

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.780
– But I still pulled it off.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.280
Ok – do you have any questions for us?

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:32.920
Yes - about your ethical policy and your carbon
footprint...I was wondering whether you are

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:38.440
planning to reduce your carbon footprint and
whether all your products are ethically sourced?

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:43.320
That’s a very good question...and that’s
something we’re moving towards at the moment...

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.530
Ok, well, let’s move on to the presentation...

